baryonic-limit-7494c82c.conceptual_definition:
The baryonic limit, represented by the baryon-to-photon ratio (
        \( \eta \)
      ), is a fundamental dimensionless cosmological parameter that quantifies the relative abundance of baryons (protons and neutrons) compared to photons in the observable universe. This ratio is crucial for understanding the conditions of the early universe, particularly during Big Bang Nucleosynthesis (BBN) and the formation of the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB). It reflects the outcome of baryogenesis, the hypothesized process that led to the observed asymmetry between matter and antimatter.

geometric-foundation-849509ea.conceptual_definition:
This formula defines the invariant spacetime interval (
        \(ds\)
        ) in terms of the proper time interval (
        \(d\tau\)
        ) for a timelike path in special relativity. It establishes a fundamental relationship between the time measured by an observer moving with an object (proper time) and the invariant interval that all inertial observers agree upon in Minkowski spacetime.
